<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Palpable right breast mass, clinically suspicious for cancer, in a postmenopausal woman of 59. Diagnostic mammography and ultrasound are booked this week, with core biopsy if indicated. Please see her once the results are available to plan surgery. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent suspected-cancer pathway, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>If the biopsy confirms cancer, would you recommend breast-conserving surgery or mastectomy, and is further imaging needed before deciding?</item><item>How should her axilla be staged?</item><item>Should germline genetic testing be done before surgery?</item><item>What anesthesia and pain-control plan would you advise, given her fear of the operation?</item><item>Should reconstruction be discussed, and what else should happen before and after surgery?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>A heavy, dull ache in the right breast for about a year and a firm lump she noticed two months ago. On examination a 2.5 cm hard, irregular, mobile mass at 10 o&apos;clock in the right breast, with no skin or nipple change and no axillary or supraclavicular nodes; left breast normal. Last screening mammogram three years ago. Menopause at 51; no hormone therapy; one pregnancy, with her only child born when she was 42. Never smoker; little alcohol. No regular medicines; no known allergy. Family history limited: her mother died when she was ten. She is very frightened of an operation and asks that every step be explained to her in advance and that she be warned before anything is done [S]</paragraph><list><item>A heavy, dull ache in the right breast for about a year and a firm lump she noticed two months ago. [S]</item><item>Dreads an operation; wants every step explained in advance and to be warned before anything is done. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Frances Burney was born in King&apos;s Lynn, Norfolk, on June 13, 1752. [D]</item><item>Burney&apos;s mother died in 1762, when Burney was about ten. [D]</item><item>Burney married Alexandre d&apos;Arblay, whom she met in 1793, and her only child, Alexander, was born in December 1794, when she was 42. In April 1802 she and her son joined her husband in France, where they lived for about ten years. [D]</item><item>Burney was a novelist, diarist, and playwright. Her first novel, Evelina, was published anonymously in January 1778, and she published four novels between 1778 and 1814. [D]</item><item>In her letter Burney wrote that about August 1810 she began to be troubled by &quot;a small pain in my breast,&quot; heavy rather than acute, which increased week by week. She resisted examination for some months; physicians and surgeons then treated her for months without success, the hardness of the affected spot increased, and her right arm was kept from use for many months. She noted that the breast was not discolored and not much larger than the other. [D]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

